Figure 1. (a) SEM image of as-synthesized Cd-containing Fe-DABCO-TPA precursors. (b) SEM micrograph, (c) PXRD pattern and (d) \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherm analysis of the Fe-C-N750 non-PGM catalyst.

Inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) analysis revealed nearly complete removal of Cd after the pyrolysis with only ca. \(0.4\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) of Cd in the produced Fe-C-N750 catalyst. By comparison, \(15.4\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) of Zn was found in the ZIF-8/Fe750 sample following the same process. This difference in the amount of residual sacrificial metal in the catalyst could largely be attributed to the difference in boiling point \((\sim\!150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) between Cd and Zn. Figure 1d shows the nitrogen isotherm of the Fe-N-C750 non-PGM catalyst. The Brunauer-EmmetTeller (BET) analysis exhibited a specific surface area of 431 \(\mathbf{m}^{2}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}\), indicating the formation of a high surface-area catalyst after the synthesis.

The ORR activities of the Fe-C-N750 and ZIF-8/Fe750 reference catalysts were evaluated by the three-electrode rotating disk electrode (RDE) technique. The RDE tests were performed under the following conditions: \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\)-saturated \(0.1\mathrm{~M~HClO}_{4}\), disk rotation rate of \(900\,\mathrm{rpm}\), and catalyst loading of \(1\,\mathrm{mg\,cm}^{-2}\). Figure 2a shows the staircase voltammogram of these two nonPGM ORR catalysts. The Fe-C-N750 catalyst exhibited a half-wave potential \(\left(\mathrm{E}_{1/2}\right)\) of \(0.70\,\mathrm{~V~}\) in the RDE testing. In comparison, the ZIF-8/Fe750 catalyst was essentially inactive. This result suggests that catalyst activation requires the removal of most of the SM.9 When the pyrolysis temperature was increased to \(950\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the \(\mathrm{E}_{1/2}\) value became \(0.80\,\mathrm{~V~}\), an increase of \(100\,\mathrm{mV}\). The Koutecky-Levich analysis indicates that the Fe-C-N750 electrocatalyst promoted a four-electron pathway in the ORR (Figure 2b). These results suggest that both the choice of SMs and the temperature of pyrolysis play important roles in determining the ORR performance of the resultant non-PGM electrocatalysts.

