The membrane electrode assembly (MEA) test was also carried out to assess the performance of the Fe-C-N750 electrocatalyst in a single fuel cell configuration (Figure 2c-d). Our study shows that at the ionomer loading of \(45\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) the Fe-C-N750 electrocatalyst exhibited a current density of \(44\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\) at a voltage of \(0.87\,\mathrm{V}\) (iR-free), \(0.03\,\mathrm{V}\) away from the DOE 2020 ORR catalyst. At the cell voltage of \(0.80\,\mathrm{V}\), the MEA made with the Fe-C-N750 catalyst exhibited a current density of \(157\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\). The power density of the MEA made using the Fe-C-N750 ORR electrocatalyst reached \(680\,\mathrm{mW\,cm}^{-2}\) for a single cell in the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}\) MEA testing (Figure S4).

